WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:58.700
[Music]

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.200
[Narrator:] In man, observations on the distribution of traits in families or kindreds,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.700
in so-called pedigree pattern, is the main method of genetic study.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.860
Since critical matings cannot be made by design, the human geneticist must rely on collections of families

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.060
for information relating to Mendel's two laws,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.460
that of segregation, and that of independent assortment.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:28.920
The pedigree method may also provide information on the two related matters of allelism and linkage.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.000
Single-factor inheritance is studied by pedigree patterns.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.800
Study of a particular trait in a family usually begins with an affected person,

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.260
who is referred to as the proband or the propositus,

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:50.040
or sometimes as the index case and is indicated by an arrow in pedigree charts.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.120
Males are represented by squares and females by circles.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.020
Although the astrologic symbols are preferred by some.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:12.000
Marital lines are indicated thus, and double lines are used if the mating is consanguineous, as, for example, between first cousins.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:18.000
Usually persons with the trait in question are indicated by filled-in symbols.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.960
More-complicated devices can be used to indicate the presence of multiple traits

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.160
or to indicate the several manifestations of a single syndrome.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.700
Traits and rare diseases of man are distributed in families in characteristic patterns,

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.140
which are in accordance with Mendel's law of segregation.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:49.040
The specific pattern is dependent on whether the responsible gene is located on one of the autosomes, or on an X chromosome.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.633
It is also dependent on whether the gene in single dose, that is in the heterozygote state,

00:03:56.633 --> 00:03:59.899
suffices to produce the phenotype under study.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:06.080
Or, whether the gene must be present in double dosage, or homozygous state for development of the trait.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:11.900
Traits observable in the heterozygous person are termed dominant.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:18.100
Whereas those which occur only in persons homozygous for the given gene are termed recessive.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.120
Autosomal dominant inheritance is illustrated by the kindred with brachydactyly studied by Farabee

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.020
soon after the rediscovery of Mendelism.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:36.220
The trait occurs in successive generations. And since it is a rare condition, only one parent has it.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.940
Among the offspring of a man or woman with brachydactyly, on the average,

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:45.080
half the sons and half the daughters are affected.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.860
This result is expected when the responsible gene is located on one of a pair of autosomes.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.020
Since only one of each pair is contributed to a given offspring by the affected parent.

00:04:58.266 --> 00:05:05.020
The chromosome given to a child may, with equal likelihood be either the one bearing the mutant gene for brachydactyly,

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.000
or the one bearing the normal or wild type gene.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:15.060
Classical achondroplastic dwarfism also behaves as an autosomal dominant trait.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.220
The family shown here is of particular interest

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.560
because an achondroplastic woman bore three children,

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.080
each by a different husband.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.980
Two of the three children were affected.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:35.433
Dominant traits tend to show rather wide variability.

00:05:35.433 --> 00:05:40.499
The degree of severity is referred to as the expression of the trait.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.400
When the expression is reduced to the point that persons with the gene

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.300
cannot be distinguished from those without it,

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.533
then the trait is said to be non-penetrant.

00:05:50.533 --> 00:05:58.433
Expression of a dominant trait as observed in a large series of cases is described by a bell-shaped curve.

00:05:58.433 --> 00:06:02.999
The largest number of cases have an intermediate grade of severity.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.233
Some are very severe, some very mild.

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:11.033
These variations may be the effects of modifier genes.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.400
Environmental factors also influence the expression of gene action.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.800
Like dominant traits, autosomal recessive traits such as albinism,

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.700
affect both males and females.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:31.566
Usually both parents are phenotypically normal, but both are carriers, that is heterozygous for the mutant gene in question.

00:06:31.566 --> 00:06:34.940
And by special tests may show mild abnormality.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.960
Since related individuals are more likely to be heterozygous for the same rare mutant gene,

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:48.420
consanguineous matings are more likely to result in offspring affected by a recessive trait.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:54.340
The more rare the recessive trait, the higher is the proportion of parental matings,

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.080
which are found to be consanguineous.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:03.780
For a rare condition, such as albinism, the proportion of parents who are first cousins may be as high as 10 percent,

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:09.933
and much higher consanguinity rates have been observed with even rarer traits.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:16.300
In the case of very rare recessive disorders, parental consanguinity may be the first clue

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.000
to its inherited nature.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:29.040
Among the offspring of two heterozygous parents, the risk of any one child's being homozygous and affected is one in four.

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:36.600
The chance of being heterozygous is two in four, or one-half.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.020
And the chance of being free of the mutant gene is one in four.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:48.620
Shown here is the pedigree of an inbred kindred in which seven cases of the Crigler-Najjar syndrome

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:51.000
have occurred in three related sib-ships.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:58.860
Jaundice is a leading feature of this syndrome, an inborn defect in the excretion of bilirubin.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.120
It is inherited as an autosomal recessive.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:09.060
In one section of this pedigree, studies showed that heterozygotes have a reduced ability

00:08:09.066 --> 00:08:12.899
to conjugate bilirubin.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:18.700
A second autosomal recessive disorder, the Morquio syndrome, also occurred in this kindred.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:33.100
If an individual affected by a recessive trait marries a homozygous normal person,

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:35.400
none of the children will be affected.,

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:37.980
but all will be heterozygous carriers.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:43.300
If an individual affected by a recessive trait marries a heterozygote,

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:47.000
then the expectation is that half the children will 
be affected.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:51.000
The pedigree pattern superficially resembles autosomal dominance.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:59.800
Previously there were thought to be two types of alkaptonuria, a more common form inherited as a recessive,

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.100
and a rarer form, inherited as a dominant.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.040
This pedigree of alkaptonuria suggested autosomal dominant inheritance.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:15.080
However, on closer study, it was found that in at least two successive generations, affected persons married a relative.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.700
It is likely that the normal spouse was in each case a heterozygote,

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:24.020
and the inheritance is indeed recessive.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:30.080
Dominance and recessiveness are of course attributes of the trait, not of the gene.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.060
As a matter of convenience, however, geneticist sometimes speak of a dominant gene or a recessive gene.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:46.160
Some traits are determined by genes carried on the x chromosome and are called sex-linked,

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.080
or more precisely, x-linked.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:56.060
Such traits have the characteristic that transmission from father to son does not occur.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.900
This follows directly from the fact that the x chromosome in the male is transmitted to none of the sons,

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.960
but is transmitted to every daughter.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.566
In females, x-linked traits may be either dominant or recessive,

00:10:11.566 --> 00:10:15.532
again depending on whether the phenotype is observable in the heterozygote

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.180
or only in the homozygote.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:23.233
However, in males who, with only one x chromosome are homozygous,

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.100
x-linked traits are regularly expressed.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.080
Rare x-linked recessive disorders occur almost exclusively in males,

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:39.600
the condition, though not expressed in heterozygous females, is transmitted by them to half their sons.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.533
Among the children of an affected male, none of the sons is affected,

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.200
but all the daughters are heterozygous carriers.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:57.060
If an affected male marries a heterozygous carrier, then a daughter may be homozygous and hence affected.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:01.980
Note that in such families, an affected male may also occur,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:07.400
but this is not an exception to the rule of no male-to-male transmission

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:11.040
since the affected male inherited the trait through the mother.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:15.980
Hemophilia is the textbook example of an x-linked recessive trait.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.100
But pseudo-hypertrophic muscular dystrophy,

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:29.100
colorblindness, and more than 50 other traits are known to be inherited in this manner.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:36.080
In the case of x-linked dominant traits such as Vitamin D-resistant rickets,

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:43.020
heterozygous females are affected, as well as homozygous males.

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:48.080
An affected female transmits the trait to half her sons and half her daughters

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.000
just as for an autosomal dominant trait.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.940
However, an affected male transmits it to none of his sons, and to all of his daughters.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:05.100
Thus, rare x-linked dominant traits occur about twice as frequently in females as in males.

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:12.120
The examples given up to now illustrate Mendel's first law, that of segregation.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:20.000
Characteristics such as the presence or absence of sickle hemoglobin are distributed in families,

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.980
in accordance with the process of myopic segregation as outlined in the first film.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:30.560
The alternate genes which determine different forms of a given trait

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:37.040
are called alleles and occupy identical positions, or loci, on homologous chromosomes.

00:12:38.166 --> 00:12:45.360
Genes at different loci are nonalleles. The distribution of traits in families may give information

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.020
on whether particular genes are allelic or nonallelic.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:55.160
Alleles segregate. Non-alleles assort.

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:59.040
Two examples are drawn from previous film.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:03.780
Are the genes for hemoglobin S and hemoglobin C allelic,

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.080
that is, at the same genetic locus?

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:13.100
A tentative answer is provided by families in which one parent has both aberrant hemoglobins,

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:16.100
and the other has only normal hemoglobin.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:24.940
In such families, it has been found that all children inherit either hemoglobin S or hemoglobin C.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:32.020
But to date, no child has inherited both, and none has inherited neither.

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:37.000
Hemoglobin S and hemoglobin C are segregating traits.

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:41.020
Their genes are by this criterion allelic.

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:47.980
Are the genes for hemoglobin S and hemoglobin Hopkins 2 at the same locus, that is, allelic?

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:56.080
Again, critical information is provided by families in which one parent has both of the aberrant hemoglobins

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:57.000
and the other has neither.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:03.000
In this case, it is found that all four possibilities occur among the offspring.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.080
Some inherit both aberrant hemoglobins, some inherit neither,

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.800
and others inherit one or the other.

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:17.040
Here is illustrated Mendel's second law, of independent assortment.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:23.960
The genes for hemoglobin S and hemoglobin Hopkins 2 are non-allelic,

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:28.000
are at different genetic loci, possibly even on different chromosomes.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:31.020
Non-alleles, assort.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:37.040
Chemical evidence also gives information on allelism and non allelism.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:42.100
In keeping with the current concept of one gene, one polypeptide chain,

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:46.140
allelic genes determine variation in the same polypeptide chain.,

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:50.880
whereas non-allelic genes are concerned with different polypeptide chains.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.080
Even though in the same protein molecule.

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:01.833
Thus, hemoglobin S and hemoglobin C are allelic and have changes

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:05.800
in the beta polypeptide of hemoglobin A.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:11.080
Whereas hemoglobin Hopkins 2 is non-allelic with hemoglobin S and C,

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.040
and has its change in the alpha chain.

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:19.080
The chemical and genetic analyses lead to the same conclusion.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:27.700
Both allelism and non-allelism are demonstrated in this instructive pedigree of congenital deafness, or deaf-mutism,

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:29.960
an autosomal recessive trait.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.060
In the first two generations, two deaf-mutes married,

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:36.980
and in each case, all children were affected.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:42.040
In the third generation, again two deaf-mutes married.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.140
Each was affected by recessive congenital deafness,

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.020
but all their children were normal.

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:53.960
We can conclude that these parents were homozygous at different loci.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:16:02.000
This pedigree suggests the existence of at least two non-allelic genetic varieties of congenital deafness.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:07.980
Non-allelism can also be established by linkage studies.

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:16.080
For example, in some families, elliptocytosis is determined by a gene at a locus close to the locus for Rh blood groups.

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:22.040
In other families, elliptocytosis, which at present cannot be distinguished phenotypically,

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:27.000
is determined by a gene at a locus not linked to that for Rh blood group.

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:34.000
The genes for the two forms of x-linked hemophilia, classic hemophilia and Christmas disease,

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:41.000
are non-allelic, where the gene for classic hemophilia is rather close to the colorblindness locus,

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:46.300
whereas the gene for Christmas disease is located on the x chromosome at a greater distance

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:48.100
from the colorblindness locus.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:56.160
Linkage is the occurrence of two loci on the same chromosome sufficiently close together

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.040
that they do not assort independently.

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:05.960
If two loci are on separate nonhomologous chromosomes, then independent assortment does occur.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:13.000
Even if two loci are on the same chromosome pair, they may be so far apart,

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:17.100
for example, one on the long arm and one on the short arm of a long chromosome,

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:22.060
that independent assortment occurs through crossing over,

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:23.940
just as though the loci were on separate chromosomes.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:32.060
Information on linkage is provided mainly by the offspring of a doubly heterozygous person

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:36.000
and a person homozygous at the same two loci.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.900
But the gene in the double heterozygote may be either in coupling

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:43.080
with the two dominants on one chromosome,

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:45.640
and the recessives on the other,

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.000
or, in repulsion with a dominant and a recessive on each.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:54.600
In the two cases, the genetic distance between the loci is the same,

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:58.920
but the phenotypes of the offspring who result from crossing over are different.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:05.933
Linkage is illustrated by the findings in 16 sib-ships reported in the literature,

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:10.220
in which the two traits studied were Lutheran blood group and the secretor factor.

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:16.080
The secretion or non-secretion of ABO blood-group substance into the saliva.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:20.080
In each of these families, one parent was doubly heterozygous

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:25.040
and the other homozygous-recessive at both loci.

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:32.040
The genotype of the parent homozygous recessive at both loci can be only the one shown here.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:38.180
However, the genotype of the doubly heterozygous parent can be either in coupling or in repulsion.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:44.460
In these data, note the strong tendency for children in a given family to be in either one class

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:47.020
or the other.

00:18:48.766 --> 00:18:52.860
In those cases in which the doubly heterozygous parent was in coupling,

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:56.000
the children were mainly of the types on the left side.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:19:00.060
When in repulsion, the children were mainly of the types on the right side.

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:06.040
However, in each of seven families, there was at least one exceptional individual.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:12.360
For example, in family five, four of the five sibs are of expected types,

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:16.260
but the fifth must be the result of a crossover in the doubly heterozygous parent.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:25.000
The same reasoning applies to the six other families where the indicated individuals are considered to be recombinants.

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:33.080
The total number of offspring in each class is about equal, thus, no clue of linkage is provided.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.100
In all, 70 children occurred in these families.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:44.100
Of these, nine are recombinants and 61 are non-recombinants.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:52.000
Thus, the recombination fraction, chi, is nine out of 70, or 13 percent.

00:19:54.740 --> 00:20:01.020
It is concluded that the Lutheran and secretor loci are on the same chromosome and about 13 map units apart.

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:06.980
The lower the recombination fraction, the closer the two loci are on the chromosome.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:11.020
If the recombination fraction is 50 percent,

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:16.460
that is, if crossover and non-crossover progeny occur with equal frequency,

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:19.080
then independent assortment obtains.

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:24.980
The loci are either on separate chromosomes or far apart on the same chromosome.

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:33.120
Linkage between markers and rare pathologic traits can also be investigated.

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:37.600
However, the pathologic trait should be inherited as a dominant.,

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:44.060
for example, elliptocytosis, and must be identifiable in the great majority of persons heterozygous for the gene.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:51.080
In addition to the linkage of the Rh locus with that for one form of elliptocytosis,

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:55.800
linkage has been demonstrated between the ABO blood group locus and the locus

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:58.060
for the so-called nail-patella syndrome.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:03.000
The loci are thought to be about 10 map units apart.

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:09.880
With the study of linkage with these rare diseases, it is necessary to use large kindreds,

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:14.000
and all available data in each, since there are few of them.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:20.220
A method of choice in linkage analysis makes use of the principle of maximum likelihood.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:28.140
The chance of the constellation of findings in the family is computed for various values of chi,

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:29.240
the recombination fraction.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:35.120
That value of chi which gives the greatest chance is the maximum likelihood estimate.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:39.140
In the time-consuming and error-fraught computations,

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:43.240
the assistance of the digital computer is enlisted.

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:55.600
It can provide points for curves such as this, derived from a group of families in which linkage of

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:59.200
the nail-patella syndrome and the ABO blood groups was studied.

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:02.300
Two important points.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:07.180
First, linkage is quite different from blood group and disease association.

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:12.533
The association between blood group O and peptic ulcer has its basis

00:22:12.533 --> 00:22:16.633
in some physiologic peculiarity of the type O person,

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:19.220
and not in the location of genes in the same chromosome.

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:25.080
Indeed, linkage produces no permanent association of traits in the population.

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:31.120
The second point, linkage must be distinguished from syndromal relationship.

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:39.040
In the Marfan syndrome, dislocated lenses in the eye, long extremities and weakness

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:42.180
of the aorta occur together.

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:47.040
However, coincidences due, in this and most hereditary syndromes, to the fact that

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:53.020
one gene has widespread effects and not due to close linkage of several separate genes.

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:58.060
Because of the recombination between homologous chromosomes

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:05.360
through crossing over, even traits due to closely linked genes are found randomly combined after the passage

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:07.940
of a number of generations.

00:23:13.866 --> 00:23:19.700
The objective of linkage studies is to map the chromosomes of man in as detailed a manner

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:23.040
as has been achieved for drosophila and for the mouse.

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:28.040
After two loci have been demonstrated on the same chromosome,

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:33.020
it still remains to be shown which autosome carries this particular linkage.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:41.100
Cytogenetic approaches may help localize specific genes to specific chromosomes or parts of chromosomes.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:48.020
In the case of x-linked traits, linkage seeks to answer the question how far apart are two loci.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:56.060
The answer is provided by the proportion of recombinants among the sons of doubly heterozygous females.

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:04.840
Here is a tentative map of the x chromosome showing the approximate relative position of the loci for xg blood group,

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:12.000
glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, colorblindness, hemophilia a, and others.

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:19.780
The cornerstone of population genetics is the Hardy-Weinberg principle,

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:26.060
that the relative frequency of genotypes remains constant from one generation to the next,

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:31.980
unless disturbed by factors such as mutation, selection, drift, and gene flow.

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:37.020
It should be noted that whether a condition is inherited as a dominant

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:40.040
has nothing to do with its frequency in the population.

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:49.020
Consider a two-allele system which we will indicate as big A for the dominant gene, and little a for its recessive allele.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:57.040
The frequency of the alleles can be represented by p and q respectively, with p plus q equals one.

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:11.000
The frequencies of the three genotypes possible with two alleles are p squared for big A, big A, 2pq for big A little a,

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:15.120
and q squared for little a, little a.

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:22.880
Hardy and Weinberg directed thinking along lines of gene frequency

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:26.100
as the pertinent variable in population genetics.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:37.920
If one collects from a population all cases of albinism, an autosomal recessive, and finds one case among each 10,000 persons,

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:45.080
this means a genotype frequency, q squared, of .0001.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:53.020
Q, the frequency of the gene little a, is the square root of this, or .01.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:05.020
The frequency of the dominant allele a is 1 minus .01 or .99.

00:26:06.840 --> 00:26:12.060
Note that the number of heterozygous carriers, big a, little a, can then be estimated.

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:23.940
This value, 2pq, is 2 times .01, times .99, or about .02.

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:30.060
One person in about 50 carries the gene for albinism.

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:37.040
The relatively high frequency of carriers of recessive traits as compared with affected is demonstrated.

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:43.060
Before considering the factors which disturb the Hardy Weinberg equilibrium,

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:48.040
we should point out the problem of segregation analysis of rare recessive traits.

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:54.100
One collects families that have at least one child affected by the trait.

00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:58.100
All of the affected persons are likely to have two normal parents,

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:03.160
both of whom are, however, heterozygous for the recessive gene.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:08.040
Among the children of two heterozygous parents, one-fourth are affected.

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:13.433
However, when all sibs in the ascertained families are added up,

00:27:13.433 --> 00:27:18.099
it is found that an appreciably higher proportion than 25 percent is affected.

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:21.566
This is due to bias of ascertainment.

00:27:21.566 --> 00:27:29.100
Those parents who were both heterozygous, but who were fortunate enough to escape having an affected child, were not ascertained.

00:27:30.820 --> 00:27:35.000
Consider two-child families with both parents heterozygous for a rare recessive gene.

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:44.940
In 16 such families, the first child will be affected in four, the second child will be affected also in four out of the 16.

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:52.080
But, since this is an independent event, one expects that in only one family will both children be affected.

00:27:55.400 --> 00:28:02.060
When we undertake to collect such families in which both parents are heterozygotes for a rare recessive gene,

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:08.180
we can usually recognize only those families which have at least one child who is affected.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:14.140
Of two-child families, only seven out of 16 can be ascertained.

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:19.000
In six of the seven families ascertained, one child will be affected.

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.080
In one of the seven, both will be affected.

00:28:24.620 --> 00:28:28.960
When we then assemble the 14 children from the seven ascertained families,

00:28:28.966 --> 00:28:33.032
we find that eight are affected, and six unaffected.

00:28:33.033 --> 00:28:37.066
Whereas the Mendelian expectation is that one-quarter of the offspring

00:28:37.066 --> 00:28:41.099
of two heterozygous parents will be homozygous and affected,

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:46.020
we have found eight out of 14, or 57 percent, affected.

00:28:48.100 --> 00:28:51.060
If one can be sure that ascertainment is complete,

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:56.020
a correction can be applied to test for agreement with the fundamental 
quarter ratio.

00:28:56.960 --> 00:29:02.020
If, as usually obtains, ascertainment is less than complete or uncertain,

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:06.080
other or more complicated methods of analysis must be used.

00:29:07.700 --> 00:29:18.020
Factors which disturb the Hardy Weinberg equilibrium are drift, gene flow, mutation, selection.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:24.000
Genetic drift is a phenomenon which occurs in small populations.

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:30.900
By chance, the gene frequencies in successive generations may deviate sharply from those

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:32.180
in earlier generations.

00:29:33.940 --> 00:29:40.020
The smaller the population, the greater is the chance of large random shifts in gene frequencies.

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:44.000
Genetic drift is nothing more than sample variance.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:51.000
Just as in experimental data, the variance is greater in studies involving smaller numbers of observations.

00:29:53.960 --> 00:30:00.500
Until as relatively recently as about 7000 years ago, when agriculture was developed on a wide scale,

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:03.920
the human species consisted of small nomadic hunting groups.

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:10.880
Genetic drift was probably a potent factor in changing the genetic constitution of man

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:14.080
and accounting for geographic differences observed today.

00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:23.020
Gene flow results in changes in the genetic constitution of populations by adding new genes

00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:28.040
through migration, contact with invading armies and miscegenation.

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:33.120
Gene flow from European to African peoples has occurred in the United States,

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:36.040
through the period of over 300 years.

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:42.000
Several genes occur in Africans south of the Sahara in relatively high frequency,

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:46.080
but are rare or absent in Europeans.

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:53.060
These include a particular Rh type, several other unusual blood groups, and a particular gamma globulin type.

00:30:54.940 --> 00:31:01.060
Estimates using these markers indicate that the present-day American negro has about 30 percent of his genes

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:03.000
derived from European ancestors.

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:08.880
Mutation occurring during gametogenesis in the parent generation

00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:13.900
will of course change, at least very slightly, the genotype frequencies in the offspring generation.

00:31:15.440 --> 00:31:20.920
Mutation is a relatively rare event, but nonetheless provides the raw material of evolution.

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:29.060
It is the basis on which selection and the other factors discussed here mold the genetic constitution of the human species.

00:31:31.520 --> 00:31:34.940
Crude methods for estimating mutation rates in man are available.

00:31:36.260 --> 00:31:38.940
Although such estimates are subject to many inaccuracies,

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:46.080
the rates which have been determined are of the same order of frequency as have been found in experimental forms.

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:49.960
About 1 mutation per locus per 100,000 gametes.

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:56.980
In other species, three classes of influences have been demonstrated to cause an increase in mutation rates.

00:31:58.040 --> 00:32:01.960
These are ionizing radiations, chemicals and heat.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:04.940
Presumably the same factors operate in man.

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:12.100
Selection is a fourth factor which tends to disturb the Hardy Weinberg equilibrium.

00:32:13.366 --> 00:32:18.560
Fitness in the biological or Darwinian sense is defined in terms

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:20.980
of the contribution made to the genes of the succeeding generation.

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:28.100
Selection is differential fitness according to the genotype of the organism.

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:31.040
Selection can operate on the haploid gamete.

00:32:31.940 --> 00:32:37.960
For example, in a heterozygous male, the sperms which carry one allele may be at a disadvantage

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:40.980
as compared to those which carry the other.

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:46.980
Furthermore, selection can operate on the diploid organism at any stage from that of the earliest zygote,

00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:50.100
to the time that the reproductive span is completed.

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:58.080
The clearest example of the operation of a specific environmental factor in selection with regard to specific phenotypes,

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:02.060
is provided by malaria and the sickle trait.

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:07.040
The high frequency of the sickle gene in some populations,

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:12.080
despite the loss of genes in the homozygous SS person with sickle cell anemia,

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:15.200
suggests an advantage of the heterozygote.

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:22.060
In these populations, sickling is a polymorphism as defined by Professor EB Ford.

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:28.080
The occurrence together in the same habitat of two or more forms of a species

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:34.040
in such proportions that the rarest of them cannot be maintained by recurrent mutation.

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:38.020
Sickling is furthermore a balance polymorphism.

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:45.060
In some populations, the frequency of the sickle gene is .40.

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:52.020
This means that .4 squared, or 16 per 100 persons

00:33:52.860 --> 00:33:57.020
have sickle cell anemia, and die at a young age with loss of these genes.

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:04.100
The frequency of the sickle gene would fall from 40 percent to a value below 5 percent

00:34:04.860 --> 00:34:09.120
in 18 generations if it were not for a balancing factor.

00:34:10.260 --> 00:34:17.060
Malaria in this population kills both AA and SA individuals early in life,

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:22.080
but many more AA than SA individuals die.

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:30.100
The result is that relatively speaking, more SA persons than AA persons survive to reproduce.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:41.260
At equilibrium, sickle genes lost in SS persons are balanced by the sickle genes saved by advantage of the SA persons.

00:34:43.660 --> 00:34:48.100
Evolution is change in the genetic constitution of the species.

00:34:49.660 --> 00:34:57.600
The factors which have been discussed as influencing gene frequency: mutation, selection, drift,

00:34:57.600 --> 00:35:02.240
and gene flow are the collaborating forces in evolution.

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:09.040
The different genetic characteristics of the races which make up the one species, man,

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:13.160
are the product of these evolutionary forces.

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:46.500
[Music]

